  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe are minimizing the maximum overlap integral for a step function h(x) on the interval [0,2] with integral 1. The current parent solution has a C5 value of 0.380861. Our approach uses a replacement process between time-frequency and time-scale representations, combined with optimal transport to match mass distributions. We replace the coordinate descent with a gradient-based optimization using the Adam algorithm, and incorporate a penalty term for discontinuities. The method can be seen as balancing time locality (to minimize overlap at nearby shifts) and frequency content (to reduce correlation at larger shifts). We first iteratively refine the solution by applying a transformation matrix W that shifts between coarser and finer resolutions, then use a local optimization procedure that minimizes the maximum correlation explicitly.\n\nMathematical specification:\nLet h be a function that can be represented as a vector of n samples on [0,2] with dx=2/n. Constraints: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, \u03a3h[i] = n/2.\nWe approximate the correlation integral as:\nC5 = max_{k} dx * \u03a3_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] * (1 - h[i+k])\n\nThe minimization problem is non-convex, so we use a two-phase approach:\n1. Coarse-to-fine optimization using a space-frequency separation matrix W:\n   h_new = \u03a3_{m=1}^{M} a_m * W_m * h_old\n   where a_m are sparse vectors that incorporate localized time-frequency adjustments.\n\n2. Local optimization using ADAM:\n   Minimize L = max_k [\u03a3_i h[i] * (1-h[i+k])] \n   with penalty \u03b7 * \u03a3_i |\u2207h[i]|^2 (to prevent too rapid changes)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Parent validation:\n   - [handling code provided ensure initial solution satisfies constraints]\n   - Convert parent to a candidate if available\n\n2. Core update search loop:\n   a. Initialize h with parent if available, else use uniform function h[i] = 0.5 for all i.\n   b. Compute current_c = max_k np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * (2.0/n)\n\n   c. Initial coarse-to-fine transformation:\n      - Define a matrix W consisting of two-dimensional filters: each row W_m corresponds to a synthetic shift vector\n      - For each shift s in [-n,2*n-2], build a sparse vector representing correlation at lag s\n\n   d. Forward transform: h_ = W * h\n      - Apply projection: h_projected = project_feasible(h_, n)\n\n   e. Gradient calculation:\n      - Forward difference estimates for gradient of correlation term with respect to h[i]\n      - Use penalty term for discontinuities\n\n   f. Adam optimization:\n      - Adam parameters: alpha=0.001, beta1=0.9, beta2=0.999, epsilon=1e-8\n      - Stochastic gradient steps with minibatch indices from uniform distribution\n\n3. Implementation fine details:\n   - Transform dimensions maintained: h stays length n\n   - Temperature parameter tau for tiebreakers in gradient alignment\n   - Limit maximum gradient norm to 1.0\n   - 50,000 function evaluations with early termination if plateau observed\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Primary hyperparameter: initial learning rate alpha=0.001\n   - Variations: \n     * Stochastic early termination\n     * Momentum variants\n     * use of different transformation matrices\n     * separate Adam phases for time and scale domains\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Estimated 750 calls of objective function per resolution level\n   - 3 resolution levels: [800, 1600, 3200] samples (doubling each time)\n   - Early termination if candidate improves by less than 1e-8 for 20 steps\n   - Fallback solution preserves best candidate ever found\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Check final h for bounds 0-1 and sum n/2\n   - Re-evaluate C5 for final solution with np.correlate(..., mode='full')*(2.0/n)\n   - Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) where:\n        h_values: numpy array of function values\n        c5_bound: scalar float of objective value\n        n_points: integer length of candidate (takes the finest resolution)\n\nSearching starts at n=1600 samples. The initial candidate from parent is thresholded at 0.5 before optimization.\n\nImplementation status: Requires building the transformation matrices and handling the constraint with projection at every step. The Adam optimizer is modified to maintain the constraint automatically via the projection and a penalty term.\n\nNote: The exact formulation of the correlation function is preserved (np.correlate(..., mode='full')) as provided by the task.",
